WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.950
Dead reckoning has rules about when you plot, and the exam follows them.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.950
Plot a DR every hour, on the hour.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.800
At every change of course or speed.

4
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.050
At the time of every fix or running fix.

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.775
And at the time you get a single line of position.

6
00:00:18.025 --> 00:00:20.225
Then the most important rule.

7
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:24.125
Whenever you get a fix, start a new DR track from it.

8
00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:26.700
Why throw away the old track?

9
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.500
Because the fix is better information.

10
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:37.600
Your old DR has been collecting error since the last fix: current, wind, a helmsman who wandered.

11
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.100
The fix wipes the slate clean.

12
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:46.200
These rules come from Bowditch, the standard reference, so exam problems are built on them.
